{"id":"65d56037-bd5d-4cbb-93f0-4022e678f3b0","object":"chat.completion","created":1785006044,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    names = [s['name'] for s in segments]\\n    importances = [s['importance'] for s in segments]\\n    minima = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\\n    capacities = [s['capacity'] for s in segments]\\n    sum_min = sum(minima)\\n    sum_cap = sum(capacities)\\n    if budget < sum_min or budget > sum_cap:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget out of feasible range\\\")\\n    allocations = list(minima)\\n    remaining = budget - sum_min\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_score = -1.0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if allocations[i] < capacities[i]:\\n                score = importances[i] / (allocations[i] + 1)\\n                if score > best_score:\\n                    best_score = score\\n                    best_idx = i\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"No capacity left but budget remains\\\")\\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    total = sum(allocations)\\n    by_name = {names[i]: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    return {\\n        \\\"allocations\\\": allocations,\\n        \\\"by_name\\\": by_name,\\n        \\\"total\\\": total\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that implements the described algorithm. The case payload has fields: \"segments\" (list of objects with name, importance, minimum, capacity), \"budget\" (integer). The task: allocate budget exactly, start each segment at its minimum, then allocate remaining budget one token at a time to the segment with highest importance / (next allocated position). Importance is given; next allocated position is current allocation + 1 (since we are about to allocate the next token). Break ties by original order (index in segments list). Return object with \"allocations\" (list of integers in same order as segments), \"by_name\" (dict mapping segment name to allocation integer), \"total\" (sum of allocations).\n\nConstraints: budget must be >= sum of minima and <= sum of capacities. Otherwise raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation details: iterate segments, compute min sum and capacity sum. If budget out of range, raise ValueError. Initialize allocations = [min for each]. remaining = budget - sum(min). Then while remaining > 0: for each segment i, if allocations[i] < capacity, compute score = importance[i] / (allocations[i] + 1). Pick index with max score; if tie, pick smaller index. Increment allocations[i] and decrement remaining. Use heap? Could just loop each iteration since small number of segments. Simpler: loop remaining times, each time compute scores.\n\nAfter loop, compute total = sum(allocations). Build by_name dict. Return dict.\n\nEdge cases: If budget exactly equals sum of minima, no loop; allocations all minima.\n\nWe must not mutate case; use copy of segments? Not needed as we only read. But we create new allocations list.\n\nNow produce final JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the source code as a JSON-escaped string. Ensure no extra characters.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    segments = case['segments']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    n = len(segments)\n    # Extract lists\n    names = [s['name'] for s in segments]\n    importances = [s['importance'] for s in segments]\n    minima = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\n    capacities = [s['capacity'] for s in segments]\n    \n    sum_min = sum(minima)\n    sum_cap = sum(capacities)\n    if budget < sum_min or budget > sum_cap:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget out of feasible range\")\n    \n    allocations = list(minima)  # copy\n    remaining = budget - sum_min\n    \n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_idx = -1\n        best_score = -1.0\n        for i in range(n):\n            if allocations[i] < capacities[i]:\n                score = importances[i] / (allocations[i] + 1)\n                if score > best_score:\n                    best_score = score\n                    best_idx = i\n                # tie: if equal, keep lower index (since we iterate in order and only update on >)\n        if best_idx == -1:\n            # Should not happen because total capacity >= budget, but safety\n            raise ValueError(\"No capacity left but budget remains\")\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\n        remaining -= 1\n    \n    total = sum(allocations)\n    by_name = {names[i]: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\n    return {\n        \"allocations\": allocations,\n        \"by_name\": by_name,\n        \"total\": total\n    }\n```\n\nNote: The output schema requires \"by_name\" to have keys from case.segments[*].name, which we are doing.\n\nNow we need to output JSON with single key \"implementation\". The value must be a JSON string (properly escaped). We'll produce the full JSON response.\n\nWe'll write the Python code as a string, escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's compose the response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1496,"completion_tokens":1162,"total_tokens":2658,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":820},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":88},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}